{"name":"Iridium","hex":"#3d3c3a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"iridium","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A chemical element with an atomic number of 77: a very hard, brittle, silvery-white transition metal of the platinum group.","An atom of this element."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/iridium"}],"entries":[{"title":"Iridium","blocks":[{"text":"Iridium is a chemical element; it has the symbol Ir and atomic number 77. This very hard, brittle, silvery-white transition metal of the platinum group is considered the second-densest naturally occurring metal, with a density of 22.56 g/cm3 (0.815 lb/cu in) as defined by experimental X-ray crystallography. 191Ir and 193Ir are the only two naturally occurring isotopes of iridium, as well as the only stable isotopes; the latter is the more abundant. It is one of the most corrosion-resistant metals, even at temperatures as high as 2,000 °C (3,600 °F)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Iridium_%2877_Ir%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Iridium_%2877_Ir%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iridium","wikidata":"Q877"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Iridium2.jpg/500px-Iridium2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Iridium2.jpg/500px-Iridium2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"One troy ounce (31.1035 grams) of arc-melted iridium","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIridium2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/IrCl3%28aq%29x.jpg/500px-IrCl3%28aq%29x.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/IrCl3%28aq%29x.jpg/1280px-IrCl3%28aq